# LF everywhere: in the repository, in the working tree, and on every platform.
#
# Git already stores text as LF whatever this file says, so CRLF in the working tree was only
# ever a conversion layer, and nothing here needs it. `eol=lf` makes the checkout match the
# storage, which means one representation instead of two.
#
# The explicit `eol=lf` rather than a bare `text=auto` is deliberate: `text=auto` alone gives
# each platform its native ending, so a Windows clone would get CRLF and fail the
# `end_of_line = lf` check that CI gates on, for a reason invisible in the diff.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touched by line-ending normalization.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.snk binary
*.nupkg binary
*.snupkg binary

# Shell scripts must keep LF or they will not run. Covered by the rule above, but stated so
# that a future change to it cannot quietly break them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The genuine exception, for whenever one appears: cmd.exe mis-parses an LF-only batch file
# around labels and goto, so these are the one thing that must stay CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
